# Documentation Writing Style

Documentation here is written for a person who has to make a decision — and for an agent that has
to avoid making a bad one. That single goal sets the voice: conceptual first, narrative, honest
about what we gave up.

Lead with the rationale. A reader can recover the mechanics from code in five minutes; what they
cannot recover is why this shape and not the obvious alternative. Go deep there, and reserve
implementation detail for the places where it clarifies rather than clutters.

Prefer flowing paragraphs over bullet lists. Bullets are for things that are genuinely a list —
roles, statuses, links. When a bullet list is really three compressed sentences, write the three
sentences. Avoid rigid Context / Decision / Rationale / Consequences scaffolding unless the
document type requires it.

Name the trade-offs. Every choice costs something, and a document that hides the cost is a
document the next person will stop trusting. "The trade-off is…" and "we pay X to get Y" are the
honest phrasings — use them, and never pretend a decision was free.

Write living docs in the present tense, describing how the system behaves today. Do not narrate
history in a living doc: no "we used to", no "this will change once". That belongs to an ADR or to
the tracker. Use inclusive "we" for decisions the team owns.

A few phrases earn their keep because they tell the reader what kind of sentence is coming. "The
key insight is…" marks the central idea. "This is critical to understand" marks something easy to
get wrong. "Now," turns toward a nuance. Use them when they are true, not as decoration.

Pick the register before drafting, and do not mix two registers in one document. For the register
table, the signature phrases, the length targets, and a worked before-and-after rewrite, see
`.cursor/skills/documentation-writing/references/voice-dna.md`.
